## Proof

**Yes, it is possible.**

**Construction.** Let $V \subset \mathbb{R}$ be a Vitali set, i.e. a set of representatives of the equivalence classes of $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}$ restricted to $[0,1]$. It is classical that $V$ is not Lebesgue ($L^1$) measurable.

Define
$$A := V \times \{0\} \subset \mathbb{R}^2.$$

**Claim 1. $A$ is $L^2$-measurable.**

The set $A$ is contained in the line $\mathbb{R} \times \{0\}$, which has two-dimensional Lebesgue measure
$$\lambda_2(\mathbb{R} \times \{0\}) = 0.$$
Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}^2$ is complete: every subset of a null set is measurable. Hence $A$, being a subset of the null set $\mathbb{R}\times\{0\}$, is $L^2$-measurable (with $\lambda_2(A)=0$).

**Claim 2. The projection of $A$ onto the first coordinate is not $L^1$-measurable.**

The projection is
$$\pi_1(A) = \{x \in \mathbb{R} : \exists\, y \in \mathbb{R},\; (x,y) \in A\} = \{x \in \mathbb{R} : (x,0) \in V\times\{0\}\} = V.$$

Since $V$ is a Vitali set, $V$ is not Lebesgue measurable in $\mathbb{R}$, i.e. $\pi_1(A)$ is not $L^1$-measurable.

**Remark on why this does not contradict the Borel case.** The projection of a *Borel* set is always analytic (Souslin), and every analytic subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is Lebesgue measurable. The construction above escapes this only because $A$ is Lebesgue-measurable but not Borel: $A = V \times \{0\}$ is a non-Borel subset of the null set $\mathbb{R}\times\{0\}$, made measurable solely by the completeness of Lebesgue measure. Completeness lets arbitrary non-measurable sets "hide" inside null sets in the higher-dimensional space, and projection then exposes them again.

